        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.69,
            0.047,
            0.862,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "A Square and A Cube"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.086,
            0.798,
            0.133
          ],
          "content": "5. Which of the following is the greatest? Explain your reasoning. (i) $6 7 ^ { 3 }   -   6 6 ^ { 3 }$ (ii) $4 3 ^ { 3 }   -   4 2 ^ { 3 }$ (iii) $6 7 ^ { 2 }   -   6 6 ^ { 2 }$ (iv) $4 3 ^ { 2 }   -   4 2 ^ { 2 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.407,
            0.162,
            0.59,
            0.187
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "SUMMARY"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.206,
            0.823,
            0.264
          ],
          "content": "A number obtained by multiplying a number by itself is called a **square number**. Squares of natural numbers are called **perfect squares**."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.272,
            0.823,
            0.311
          ],
          "content": "All perfect squares end with 0, 1, 4, 5, 6 or 9. Squares can only have an even number of zeros at the end."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.321,
            0.824,
            0.38
          ],
          "content": "**Square root** is the inverse operation of square. Every perfect square has two integral square roots. The positive square root of a number is denoted by the symbol √ . For example, ${ \\sqrt { 9 } } \\equiv 3 ,$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.389,
            0.823,
            0.429
          ],
          "content": " A **number** obtained by multiplying a number by itself three times is called a **cube**. For example 1, 8, 27, ... ,etc., are cubes."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.437,
            0.823,
            0.477
          ],
          "content": "A number is a perfect square if its prime factors can be split into two identical groups."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.485,
            0.822,
            0.525
          ],
          "content": "A number is a perfect cube if its prime factors can be split into three identical groups"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.534,
            0.761,
            0.556
          ],
          "content": " The symbol <sup>3</sup> denotes cube root. For example, $\\sqrt[3]{27}   =   3$"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.879,
            0.915,
            0.911,
            0.936
          ],
          "content": "1 7"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
